{"text": "A content delivery network works by storing cached versions of website content on a distributed network of servers located in various geographic locations. When a user requests data, the network directs the request to the nearest server, known as an edge server, to minimize latency. This process reduces the physical distance data must travel, resulting in faster loading speeds and decreased bandwidth strain on the origin server.", "reasoning": "", "calls": [], "error": null, "done": {"event": "done", "stop": "stop", "usage": {"input": 52, "cache_read": 0, "output": 75, "reasoning": 0, "replayed": 0, "shadow_prefilled": 0, "shadow_background": 0, "shadow_remaining": 0, "shadow_kv_bytes": 0, "shadow_wait_us": 0, "total": 127}, "inference": {"prefill": {"tokens": 52, "total": 52, "elapsed_ms": 515}, "decode": {"tokens": 75, "elapsed_ms": 2477}}, "marker": null}, "first_text_seconds": 0.6234997760038823, "wall_seconds": 3.070799559005536, "configuration": "system_prompt", "index": 0, "prompt": "Explain how a content delivery network works.", "process_memory_kib": {"VmHWM": 14083760, "VmRSS": 184828}, "words": 67, "repeated_4gram_fraction": 0.0, "replacement_characters": 0, "longest_character_run": 2}
