{"text": "A stack follows the Last-In, First-Out (LIFO) principle, meaning the last element added is the first one removed, similar to a pile of plates. In contrast, a queue follows the First-In, First-Out (FIFO) principle, where the first element added is the first one removed, much like a line of people waiting. The primary difference lies in the order of processing: stacks prioritize the newest items, while queues prioritize the oldest items.", "reasoning": "", "calls": [], "error": null, "done": {"event": "done", "stop": "stop", "usage": {"input": 54, "cache_read": 0, "output": 97, "reasoning": 0, "replayed": 0, "shadow_prefilled": 0, "shadow_background": 0, "shadow_remaining": 0, "shadow_kv_bytes": 0, "shadow_wait_us": 0, "total": 151}, "inference": {"prefill": {"tokens": 54, "total": 54, "elapsed_ms": 505}, "decode": {"tokens": 97, "elapsed_ms": 3231}}, "marker": null}, "first_text_seconds": 0.6147134799975902, "wall_seconds": 3.8152756610070355, "configuration": "system_prompt", "index": 2, "prompt": "Explain the difference between a stack and a queue.", "process_memory_kib": {"VmHWM": 14083760, "VmRSS": 184828}, "words": 71, "repeated_4gram_fraction": 0.05882352941176472, "replacement_characters": 0, "longest_character_run": 2}
