{"text": "To understand how to explain a **Content Delivery Network (CDN)**, it helps to start with a simple analogy.\n\nImagine you live in London, and you want to order a very specific, heavy book from a small, independent bookstore located in a tiny village in the mountains of India. \n\nIf you were to order that book directly from that tiny village, you might think: *\"Well, I’ forward to reading this. I suppose I’ll have to wait a while.\"*\n\n**In this analogy, the bookstore in India is the \"Origin Server,\" and the book you are waiting for is the \"Data\" that you want to read.**\n\nIn the digital world, this is exactly what happens when you click on a link. To make this process easier, the bookstore in India has decided to partner with a logistics company. They’ to make sure that when a customer in London orders a book, the experience is seamless.\n\n**In the digital world, that logistics company is what we call a Content Delivery Network (CDN).**\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Problem: The \"Distance\" of the Internet)\nBefore we dive into how the CDN works, we have to understand the problem it was designed to solve.\n\nIn the early days of the internet, if you built a website, you would host it on a single server. Let’s say you built a beautiful website for a company called \"Global Tech\" based in San Francisco. \n\n**The problem is this:** If a student in Mumbai, India, wants to visit `www.globaltech.com`, they aren't just clicking a link. They are embarking on a journey. \n\nIf we were to look at the \"map\" of how that student’s computer is talking to the \"Global Tech\" server, we would realize that the data has to travel through undersea cables, through", "reasoning": "", "calls": [], "error": null, "done": {"event": "done", "stop": "length", "usage": {"input": 32, "cache_read": 0, "output": 384, "reasoning": 0, "replayed": 0, "shadow_prefilled": 0, "shadow_background": 0, "shadow_remaining": 0, "shadow_kv_bytes": 0, "shadow_wait_us": 0, "total": 416}, "infer